### Option B: The application expects `/health`, `/products`, etc.

If you need `/api/search/health` to become `/health`, the configuration depends on your Ingress controller.

For an NGINX Ingress Controller, one approach is to create a separate `k8s/ingress.yaml` in the search repository:

```

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: search-service
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/use-regex: "true"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /$2
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  rules:
    - http:
        paths:
          - path: /api/search(/|$)(.*)
            pathType: ImplementationSpecific
            backend:
              service:
                name: search-service
                port:
                  number: 3000

```

This example assumes that the Ingress controller supports the specified NGINX annotations and that the `search-service` Service is in the same namespace as this Ingress.

Requests are rewritten as follows:

| External request       | Search application path |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------- |
| `/api/search/health`   | `/health`               |
| `/api/search/products` | `/products`             |
| `/api/search`          | `/`                     |

Important: Do not blindly apply this rewrite annotation to an existing shared NGINX Ingress containing other services. The rewrite settings may affect other paths handled by that Ingress. Keep search on a separate Ingress resource, or use a suitable Gateway/API gateway configuration.

Also, `ingressClassName: nginx` must match your installed controller. If you are using GKE's native GCE Ingress instead, this NGINX-specific configuration will not work. Kubernetes documents that path handling and controller-specific annotations depend on the selected implementation.&#x20;

